# Azure Files SDK Helper for C#

This helper class mimics standard `System.IO.File` operations but uses the official Azure Files SDK and `DefaultAzureCredential` under the hood. 

By switching from SMB/UNC paths to this HTTPS-based approach, you eliminate the requirement for a domain-joined Windows host and active directory infrastructure. This allows you to run your C# application on completely free or low-cost hosting options like the **Azure App Service Free Tier** or **Azure Functions Free Tier**.

---

## 1. Install Required NuGet Packages

Run these commands in your project terminal to install the necessary Azure identity and storage packages:

```bash
dotnet add package Azure.Identity
dotnet add package Azure.Storage.Files.Shares
```

---

## 2. Code Implementation (`CloudFile.cs`)

Add this static helper class to your application. It parses standard Azure File Share URLs and handles secure, identity-based communication.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Azure.Identity;
using Azure.Storage.Files.Shares;

public static class CloudFile
{
    // Caches the token credential to optimize performance across calls
    private static readonly DefaultAzureCredential _credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();

    private static ShareFileClient GetFileClient(string path)
    {
        // Expected format: https://<storage_account>.file.core.windows.net/<share_name>/<folder>/<file>
        var uri = new Uri(path);
        
        // Extract the base storage account endpoint
        var accountUri = new Uri(\$"{uri.Scheme}://{uri.Host}");
        
        // Isolate the share name (the first segment after the domain)
        string shareName = uri.Segments[1].TrimEnd('/');
        
        // Reconstruct the remaining directories and file path
        string remainingPath = string.Join("", uri.Segments, 2, uri.Segments.Length - 2);

        // Initialize the clients using the platform's ambient identity
        var serviceClient = new ShareServiceClient(accountUri, _credential);
        var shareClient = serviceClient.GetShareClient(shareName);
        
        return shareClient.GetRootDirectoryClient().GetFileClient(remainingPath);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Asynchronously opens a text file, reads all text, and then closes the file.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task<string> ReadAllTextAsync(string path)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        var downloadInfo = await fileClient.DownloadAsync();
        
        using (var reader = new StreamReader(downloadInfo.Value.Content, Encoding.UTF8))
        {
            return await reader.ReadToEndAsync();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Asynchronously creates a new file, writes the specified string to it, and then closes the file. 
    /// If the target file already exists, it is overwritten.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task WriteAllTextAsync(string path, string contents)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        
        using (var stream = new MemoryStream(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(contents)))
        {
            // You must initialize the file size in Azure Files before uploading ranges
            await fileClient.CreateAsync(stream.Length);
            await fileClient.UploadRangeAsync(new Azure.HttpRange(0, stream.Length), stream);
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 3. Code Migration Example

Updating your application logic requires minimal changes. You only need to update the path string format and use the `CloudFile` class instead of `System.IO.File`.

### Before (Expensive Setup)
This approach relies on Windows OS context, SMB port 445 routing, and a domain controller ($110+/month).
```csharp
using System.IO;

string uncPath = @"\\mystorageaccount.file.core.windows.net\myfileshare\myfolder\data.txt";

// Throws access errors on standard cloud containers or free hosts
string content = File.ReadAllText(uncPath); 
```

### After (Inexpensive/Free Setup)
This approach leverages standard HTTP/HTTPS channels, allowing deployment to free compute tiers.
```csharp
using System.Threading.Tasks;

string httpPath = "https://windows.net";

// Works seamlessly on Free Azure App Services using identity tokens
string content = await CloudFile.ReadAllTextAsync(httpPath);
```





