# Azure Files SDK Helper for C# (with Binary Support)

This helper class mimics standard `System.IO.File` operations but uses the official Azure Files SDK and `DefaultAzureCredential` under the hood. 

By switching from SMB/UNC paths to this HTTPS-based approach, you eliminate the requirement for a domain-joined Windows host and active directory infrastructure. This allows you to run your C# application on completely free or low-cost hosting options like the **Azure App Service Free Tier** or **Azure Functions Free Tier**.

---

## 1. Install Required NuGet Packages

Run these commands in your project terminal to install the necessary Azure identity and storage packages:

```bash
dotnet add package Azure.Identity
dotnet add package Azure.Storage.Files.Shares
```

---

## 2. Code Implementation (`CloudFile.cs`)

Add this static helper class to your application. It parses standard Azure File Share URLs and handles secure, identity-based communication, including precise binary reading, writing, and seeking.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Azure;
using Azure.Identity;
using Azure.Storage.Files.Shares;
using Azure.Storage.Files.Shares.Models;

public static class CloudFile
{
    // Caches the token credential to optimize performance across calls
    private static readonly DefaultAzureCredential _credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();

    private static ShareFileClient GetFileClient(string path)
    {
        // Expected format: https://<storage_account>.file.core.windows.net/<share_name>/<folder>/<file>
        var uri = new Uri(path);
        
        // Extract the base storage account endpoint
        var accountUri = new Uri(\$"{uri.Scheme}://{uri.Host}");
        
        // Isolate the share name (the first segment after the domain)
        string shareName = uri.Segments[1].TrimEnd('/');
        
        // Reconstruct the remaining directories and file path
        string remainingPath = string.Join("", uri.Segments, 2, uri.Segments.Length - 2);

        // Initialize the clients using the platform's ambient identity
        var serviceClient = new ShareServiceClient(accountUri, _credential);
        var shareClient = serviceClient.GetShareClient(shareName);
        
        return shareClient.GetRootDirectoryClient().GetFileClient(remainingPath);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Reads the entire contents of a file as a byte array.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task<byte[]> ReadAllBytesAsync(string path)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        var downloadInfo = await fileClient.DownloadAsync();
        
        using (var memoryStream = new MemoryStream())
        {
            await downloadInfo.Value.Content.CopyToAsync(memoryStream);
            return memoryStream.ToArray();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Creates a new file, writes the specified byte array to it, and closes the file.
    /// If the target file already exists, it is overwritten.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task WriteAllBytesAsync(string path, byte[] bytes)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        
        // You must initialize the file size in Azure Files before uploading ranges
        await fileClient.CreateAsync(bytes.Length);
        
        using (var stream = new MemoryStream(bytes))
        {
            await fileClient.UploadRangeAsync(new HttpRange(0, bytes.Length), stream);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Reads a specific block of bytes from a file starting at a precise byte offset (Seek & Read).
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="path">The HTTPS path to the file.</param>
    /// <param name="offset">The starting byte position in the remote file (Seek position).</param>
    /// <param name="count">The number of bytes to read.</param>
    public static async Task<byte[]> ReadRangeAsync(string path, long offset, int count)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        var options = new ShareFileDownloadOptions
        {
            Range = new HttpRange(offset, count)
        };

        try
        {
            var downloadInfo = await fileClient.DownloadAsync(options);
            using (var memoryStream = new MemoryStream())
            {
                await downloadInfo.Value.Content.CopyToAsync(memoryStream);
                return memoryStream.ToArray();
            }
        }
        catch (RequestFailedException ex) when (ex.Status == 416)
        {
            // Range Not Satisfiable (e.g., trying to read past the current end of the file)
            return Array.Empty<byte>();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Overwrites or appends a block of bytes at a precise byte offset inside an existing file (Seek & Write).
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="path">The HTTPS path to the file.</param>
    /// <param name="offset">The starting byte position where writing should begin (Seek position).</param>
    /// <param name="bytes">The data buffer to write.</param>
    public static async Task WriteRangeAsync(string path, long offset, byte[] bytes)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        
        // Get current file properties to check its size boundary
        ShareFileProperties properties = await fileClient.GetPropertiesAsync();
        long requiredSize = offset + bytes.Length;

        // If the new write position stretches past the current file size, expand the file allocation first
        if (requiredSize > properties.ContentLength)
        {
            await fileClient.SetHttpHeadersAsync(requiredSize);
        }

        using (var stream = new MemoryStream(bytes))
        {
            await fileClient.UploadRangeAsync(new HttpRange(offset, bytes.Length), stream);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Appends a block of bytes directly to the end of an existing file.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task AppendBytesAsync(string path, byte[] bytes)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        ShareFileProperties properties = await fileClient.GetPropertiesAsync();
        
        long currentSize = properties.ContentLength;
        await fileClient.SetHttpHeadersAsync(currentSize + bytes.Length);

        using (var stream = new MemoryStream(bytes))
        {
            await fileClient.UploadRangeAsync(new HttpRange(currentSize, bytes.Length), stream);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Asynchronously opens a text file, reads all text, and then closes the file.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task<string> ReadAllTextAsync(string path)
    {
        var bytes = await ReadAllBytesAsync(path);
        return Encoding.UTF8.GetString(bytes);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Asynchronously creates a new file, writes the specified string to it, and then closes the file.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task WriteAllTextAsync(string path, string contents)
    {
        var bytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(contents);
        await WriteAllBytesAsync(path, bytes);
    }
}
```

=============================================

## 3. Binary & Seek Usage Example

### Reading a Specific Chunk (Seek and Read)
If you have a binary file and only need bytes `1024` through `2048`:
```csharp
string httpPath = "https://windows.net";

// Seeks to byte 1024 and reads the next 1024 bytes
byte[] dataChunk = await CloudFile.ReadRangeAsync(httpPath, offset: 1024, count: 1024);
```

### Modifying File Header/Bytes (Seek and Write)
If you need to overwrite specific data blocks (like updating a file header at byte index 0) without pulling down the whole file:
```csharp
string httpPath = "https://windows.net";
byte[] newHeader = new byte[] { 0x46, 0x49, 0x4C, 0x45 }; // "FILE" magic bytes

// Overwrites bytes starting at index 0
await CloudFile.WriteRangeAsync(httpPath, offset: 0, bytes: newHeader);
```




# Azure Files SDK Helper for C# (with Binary Support)

This helper class mimics standard `System.IO.File` operations but uses the official Azure Files SDK and `DefaultAzureCredential` under the hood. 

By switching from SMB/UNC paths to this HTTPS-based approach, you eliminate the requirement for a domain-joined Windows host and active directory infrastructure. This allows you to run your C# application on completely free or low-cost hosting options like the **Azure App Service Free Tier** or **Azure Functions Free Tier**.

---

## 1. Install Required NuGet Packages

Run these commands in your project terminal to install the necessary Azure identity and storage packages:

```bash
dotnet add package Azure.Identity
dotnet add package Azure.Storage.Files.Shares
```

---

## 2. Code Implementation (`CloudFile.cs`)

Add this static helper class to your application. It parses standard Azure File Share URLs and handles secure, identity-based communication, including precise binary reading, writing, and seeking.

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Azure;
using Azure.Identity;
using Azure.Storage.Files.Shares;
using Azure.Storage.Files.Shares.Models;

public static class CloudFile
{
    // Caches the token credential to optimize performance across calls
    private static readonly DefaultAzureCredential _credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();

    private static ShareFileClient GetFileClient(string path)
    {
        // Expected format: https://<storage_account>.file.core.windows.net/<share_name>/<folder>/<file>
        var uri = new Uri(path);
        
        // Extract the base storage account endpoint
        var accountUri = new Uri(\$"{uri.Scheme}://{uri.Host}");
        
        // Isolate the share name (the first segment after the domain)
        string shareName = uri.Segments[1].TrimEnd('/');
        
        // Reconstruct the remaining directories and file path
        string remainingPath = string.Join("", uri.Segments, 2, uri.Segments.Length - 2);

        // Initialize the clients using the platform's ambient identity
        var serviceClient = new ShareServiceClient(accountUri, _credential);
        var shareClient = serviceClient.GetShareClient(shareName);
        
        return shareClient.GetRootDirectoryClient().GetFileClient(remainingPath);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Reads the entire contents of a file as a byte array.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task<byte[]> ReadAllBytesAsync(string path)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        var downloadInfo = await fileClient.DownloadAsync();
        
        using (var memoryStream = new MemoryStream())
        {
            await downloadInfo.Value.Content.CopyToAsync(memoryStream);
            return memoryStream.ToArray();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Creates a new file, writes the specified byte array to it, and closes the file.
    /// If the target file already exists, it is overwritten.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task WriteAllBytesAsync(string path, byte[] bytes)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        
        // You must initialize the file size in Azure Files before uploading ranges
        await fileClient.CreateAsync(bytes.Length);
        
        using (var stream = new MemoryStream(bytes))
        {
            await fileClient.UploadRangeAsync(new HttpRange(0, bytes.Length), stream);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Reads a specific block of bytes from a file starting at a precise byte offset (Seek & Read).
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="path">The HTTPS path to the file.</param>
    /// <param name="offset">The starting byte position in the remote file (Seek position).</param>
    /// <param name="count">The number of bytes to read.</param>
    public static async Task<byte[]> ReadRangeAsync(string path, long offset, int count)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        var options = new ShareFileDownloadOptions
        {
            Range = new HttpRange(offset, count)
        };

        try
        {
            var downloadInfo = await fileClient.DownloadAsync(options);
            using (var memoryStream = new MemoryStream())
            {
                await downloadInfo.Value.Content.CopyToAsync(memoryStream);
                return memoryStream.ToArray();
            }
        }
        catch (RequestFailedException ex) when (ex.Status == 416)
        {
            // Range Not Satisfiable (e.g., trying to read past the current end of the file)
            return Array.Empty<byte>();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Overwrites or appends a block of bytes at a precise byte offset inside an existing file (Seek & Write).
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="path">The HTTPS path to the file.</param>
    /// <param name="offset">The starting byte position where writing should begin (Seek position).</param>
    /// <param name="bytes">The data buffer to write.</param>
    public static async Task WriteRangeAsync(string path, long offset, byte[] bytes)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        
        // Get current file properties to check its size boundary
        ShareFileProperties properties = await fileClient.GetPropertiesAsync();
        long requiredSize = offset + bytes.Length;

        // If the new write position stretches past the current file size, expand the file allocation first
        if (requiredSize > properties.ContentLength)
        {
            await fileClient.SetHttpHeadersAsync(requiredSize);
        }

        using (var stream = new MemoryStream(bytes))
        {
            await fileClient.UploadRangeAsync(new HttpRange(offset, bytes.Length), stream);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Appends a block of bytes directly to the end of an existing file.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task AppendBytesAsync(string path, byte[] bytes)
    {
        var fileClient = GetFileClient(path);
        ShareFileProperties properties = await fileClient.GetPropertiesAsync();
        
        long currentSize = properties.ContentLength;
        await fileClient.SetHttpHeadersAsync(currentSize + bytes.Length);

        using (var stream = new MemoryStream(bytes))
        {
            await fileClient.UploadRangeAsync(new HttpRange(currentSize, bytes.Length), stream);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Asynchronously opens a text file, reads all text, and then closes the file.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task<string> ReadAllTextAsync(string path)
    {
        var bytes = await ReadAllBytesAsync(path);
        return Encoding.UTF8.GetString(bytes);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Asynchronously creates a new file, writes the specified string to it, and then closes the file.
    /// </summary>
    public static async Task WriteAllTextAsync(string path, string contents)
    {
        var bytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(contents);
        await WriteAllBytesAsync(path, bytes);
    }
}
```

---

## 3. Binary & Seek Usage Example

### Reading a Specific Chunk (Seek and Read)
If you have a binary file and only need bytes `1024` through `2048`:
```csharp
string httpPath = "https://windows.net";

// Seeks to byte 1024 and reads the next 1024 bytes
byte[] dataChunk = await CloudFile.ReadRangeAsync(httpPath, offset: 1024, count: 1024);
```

### Modifying File Header/Bytes (Seek and Write)
If you need to overwrite specific data blocks (like updating a file header at byte index 0) without pulling down the whole file:
```csharp
string httpPath = "https://windows.net";
byte[] newHeader = new byte[] { 0x46, 0x49, 0x4C, 0x45 }; // "FILE" magic bytes

// Overwrites bytes starting at index 0
await CloudFile.WriteRangeAsync(httpPath, offset: 0, bytes: newHeader);
```


